 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "**Mechanism:** werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects reading from a single `contextvars.ContextVar`. Each proxy resolves the current context's object on every attribute access.\n\n**Objects**, all in `src/tessel/globals.py`:\n- `_app_var` (line 40) is the `ContextVar[AppContext]` holding the active `AppContext`. It is set and reset when an app or request context is pushed and popped.\n- `active_app` (line 44) is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"app\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.app`, the Tessel app instance.\n- `g` (line 47) is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"g\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.g`, the `_AppCtxGlobals` object.\n- `request` (line 57) is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"request\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.request`, the current request.\n- `app_ctx` (line 41), the whole `AppContext`, and `session` (line 60, `\"session\"` attribute) use the same pattern.\n\nWith no context pushed, the `ContextVar` lookup fails and the proxy raises the `unbound_message` error (`_no_app_msg` or `_no_req_msg`). This is also why `repr(tessel.g)` shows `<LocalProxy unbound>` (see `tests/test_basic.py:1514`). The design is described in `docs/appcontext.rst` (around line 174).\n\nI didn't open the context-push code, so the set/reset step is inferred from how `_app_var` is used here.",
